WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.580
A European service station on the moon will keep experiments running for years by storing solar power for the two-week lunar night. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 228. After that lunar hub come Uranus and Neptune's separate origins. Twilight clouds over gale crater and a cosmic deep dive on ice giant migration. The outpost for Advanced Scientific Investigation Station, or Oasis, is the European Space Agency designed for that shared surface hub. It will supply at least

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.860
75 watts of continuous power during the lunar day and 15 watts at night, along with communications,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.380
data handling, and storage for multiple payloads. OHB in Germany is leading the feasibility studies,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:48.160
and a systems requirement review is already underway. The station is modular and extendable,

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.320
and it could offer sheltered spots where rovers wait out the dark. One early payload,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.540
the environment, characterization, and heliophysics observatory, or echo, will test the infrastructure

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.480
while it studies the lunar environment.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.480
The layout draws on the Apollo lunar surface experiments package, or ALSEP, which connected

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.340
instruments to a central station.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.700
Oasis uses a solar array and a separate battery unit, so experiment teams no longer each haul

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.640
their own heavy-night survival hardware.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.400
Future versions could feed science or technology demonstrators farther from the lander, or

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.420
serve as rover garages through the night.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.520
The concept rides an Argonaut lander launched on a four booster Ariane 6, with communications

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.600
through the Moonlight Network starting with Lunar Pathfinder.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:43.100
ESA wants this as a recurring European lunar capability, not a one-off, scaled so several

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.060
robotic payloads can run at once.

00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:51.993
Uranus and Neptune showed different chemical makeups according to a Fizz.org space report

00:01:51.993 --> 00:01:57.593
on an international team's work. The team found Neptune more enriched in oxygen than Uranus

00:01:57.593 --> 00:02:02.773
by probing the two atmospheres indirectly. That contrast suggests the ice giants formed

00:02:02.773 --> 00:02:07.533
in different regions of the early solar system before they migrated to their current orbits.

00:02:08.053 --> 00:02:12.633
The result challenges the long-held view that the two planets share a common birthplace.

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:20.353
By measuring composition through those atmospheric observations, the researchers revealed a significant difference in oxygen content.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:28.133
Models of the primitive solar system must now account for chemically distinct formation zones rather than identical starting conditions.

00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:37.073
Uranus sits 2.9 billion kilometers from the Sun, while Neptune lies another 1.6 billion kilometers farther out.

00:02:37.593 --> 00:02:41.513
That gap is wide enough to contain the entire inner solar system twice over.

00:02:41.513 --> 00:02:46.653
The separation once looked like evidence the two worlds formed as twins in the same region.

00:02:47.153 --> 00:02:50.613
Imagine riding with the building blocks while the young solar system stirred.

00:02:51.353 --> 00:02:55.333
Closer to the sun, temperatures allowed more oxygen-bearing ices to condense.

00:02:55.933 --> 00:02:58.173
Farther out, the mix favored other compounds.

00:02:58.893 --> 00:03:03.473
A planet assembled in the oxygen-rich zone would carry a different bulk composition than

00:03:03.473 --> 00:03:04.813
one born farther away.

00:03:05.433 --> 00:03:10.553
Once giant planets began flinging one another through gravitational encounters, those original

00:03:10.553 --> 00:03:15.633
locations became scrambled. The surviving chemical fingerprints in today's atmospheres

00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:20.773
are the only record left of where each world started. The team used indirect atmospheric

00:03:20.773 --> 00:03:26.673
probing, with no probes sent into the planet some cells. We still do not know exactly how far

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:32.193
apart those birthplaces were, or what triggered the migration. Models can reproduce the current

00:03:32.193 --> 00:03:37.213
orbits, yet they cannot yet match the measured oxygen contrast without fine-tuning assumptions

00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:42.353
about the early disk. A single high precision measurement of another heavy element in both

00:03:42.353 --> 00:03:47.313
atmospheres could decide whether the planets formed on opposite sides of a chemical boundary,

00:03:47.813 --> 00:03:53.513
or whether some later process erased and expected similarity. The same data might clarify whether

00:03:53.513 --> 00:03:59.153
the migration happened early, while the disk was still gas-rich or later, after most of the gas

00:03:59.153 --> 00:04:04.593
had dissipated. Either scenario would reshape how we reconstruct the dynamical history of the outer

00:04:04.593 --> 00:04:08.233
solar system and the delivery of volatiles to the inner planets.

00:04:09.727 --> 00:04:15.447
Curiosity captured zenith movies of wispy clouds above gale crater shortly before sunset

00:04:15.447 --> 00:04:18.387
on Sol 5,037 NASA reports.

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:23.947
The clouds are bright enough to appear in raw nav cam images, even though most atmospheric

00:04:23.947 --> 00:04:26.167
clouds at the site remain too thin to see.

00:04:26.827 --> 00:04:31.487
The observations mark the start of seasonal twilight cloud activity as Mars enters its

00:04:31.487 --> 00:04:32.227
northern spring.

00:04:32.227 --> 00:04:37.607
The new Mars year began at Solar Longitude 0 degrees at the end of September on Earth.

00:04:38.007 --> 00:04:43.267
The rover wrapped up drill activities at BASC lakes while collecting additional GCMS data

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:46.727
and remote sensing observations from MASCAM and ChemCAM.

00:04:47.357 --> 00:04:52.147
The team plans to drive back toward crystal forms seen in the Choo-Choo region after finishing

00:04:52.147 --> 00:04:54.227
contact science on the drill tailings.

00:04:56.907 --> 00:05:01.597
South Australia's government reaffirmed support for its astronaut program despite shifting

00:05:01.597 --> 00:05:03.897
timelines, ABC news reports.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:08.517
The commitment keeps open a pathway for an Australian of the year candidate to reach

00:05:08.517 --> 00:05:10.457
orbit through international partnerships.

00:05:11.097 --> 00:05:15.757
The state's outback already hosted sample recoveries from Japan's Hayabusa 2 mission

00:05:15.757 --> 00:05:16.577
in 2020.

00:05:17.337 --> 00:05:21.677
The ongoing program strengthens South Australia's role in international space work.

00:05:22.137 --> 00:05:27.317
It also provides openings for Australian researchers in spacecraft recovery and technical operations.

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:36.935
Euclid detected comet C-2026-S-1, Euclid in images taken between the 16th and 24th of

00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:40.015
September, 2026, universe-today reports.

00:05:40.515 --> 00:05:45.455
The European Space Agency spacecraft was surveying galaxies to study dark matter and

00:05:45.455 --> 00:05:47.755
dark energy when the object appeared.

00:05:48.435 --> 00:05:53.255
Those wide field observations captured the new comet 810 million miles from Earth.

00:05:53.255 --> 00:05:58.455
The fine shows the telescope can pick out solar system objects alongside its primary

00:05:58.455 --> 00:05:59.895
cosmological survey.

00:06:02.383 --> 00:06:06.303
A stellar mass black hole system has been verified as a micro blazar.

00:06:06.883 --> 00:06:11.483
Universe-Today reports, the galactic counterpart to extra galactic blazar's.

00:06:11.883 --> 00:06:15.703
The object may accelerate the most energetic particles observed in the galaxy.

00:06:16.443 --> 00:06:19.443
Distant blazar's are powered by supermassive black holes.

00:06:19.903 --> 00:06:21.943
This one operates inside the Milky Way.

00:06:21.943 --> 00:06:26.823
That nearby setting offers a closer view of the same high-energy processes.

00:06:28.249 --> 00:06:34.169
McGill University astronomers measured infrared emission from gas clouds near Sagittarius A-Star,

00:06:34.549 --> 00:06:39.749
and found elevated levels of elements heavier than helium, according to Fizz.org space.

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:45.409
The composition matches stars orbiting the black hole, suggesting the gas and stars formed

00:06:45.409 --> 00:06:45.909
together.

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:50.229
The enrichment likely came from earlier generations of exploding stars.

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:56.249
The James Webb Space Telescopes High Spectral and Spatial Resolution let the team distinguish

00:06:56.249 --> 00:07:00.389
heat signatures of multiple gas clouds and measure their metal content.

00:07:01.109 --> 00:07:05.809
Nicole Ford, the lead author, noted that understanding why the black hole consumes material

00:07:05.809 --> 00:07:11.029
more slowly, requires examining the properties of available gas, dust, and stars.

00:07:11.489 --> 00:07:16.509
A companion study tested whether the gas preserved evidence of previous energy bursts from the

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:19.969
black hole, but found local conditions dominate the emission.

00:07:21.529 --> 00:07:27.949
Young tectonic landforms on the moon now add up to 10,000 mapped cracks, space.com reports,

00:07:28.509 --> 00:07:32.049
account used to measure how much the interior has contracted as it cooled.

00:07:32.749 --> 00:07:36.249
The work gives the clearest picture yet of the moon's ongoing shrinkage.

00:07:36.709 --> 00:07:41.429
Those features formed as the interior lost heat and the crust adjusted to the reduced volume.

00:07:41.429 --> 00:07:45.509
The mapping traces that long-term volume loss across the surface.

00:07:46.709 --> 00:07:52.549
Mercury still has no lander, fizz.org space notes, because extreme temperatures, high

00:07:52.549 --> 00:07:56.089
orbital speeds, and harsh radiation have blocked every attempt.

00:07:56.829 --> 00:08:01.129
Scientists argue that only a dedicated lander can resolve long-standing questions about

00:08:01.129 --> 00:08:02.869
the planet's composition and history.

00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:07.889
Reaching the innermost world demands propulsion and thermal protection beyond the systems

00:08:07.889 --> 00:08:11.809
terms used for more distant targets such as the icy moons of Saturn or Jupiter.

00:08:13.129 --> 00:08:18.289
A pair of supermassive black holes could collide on a time scale as short as 100 years,

00:08:18.989 --> 00:08:20.589
news.am tech reports.

00:08:21.409 --> 00:08:26.149
Observations suggest that coalescence would be the first predicted merger on human time scales.

00:08:26.729 --> 00:08:31.549
The short interval offers a rare chance to watch the final stages of a supermassive black hole

00:08:31.549 --> 00:08:32.369
merger directly.

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:39.509
Spanish startup UARX Space is also today addressing issues identified during the first flight

00:08:39.509 --> 00:08:45.249
of its OSIID orbital transfer vehicle, launched on the first of October aboard a SpaceX

00:08:45.249 --> 00:08:50.789
transporter mission. A study using ALMA data shows how jets from supermassive black holes

00:08:50.789 --> 00:08:56.949
can heat gas at the outer edges of galaxies and slow star formation over time. Before we go,

00:08:57.269 --> 00:09:01.909
watch for the next word on that Spanish orbital transfer vehicle as the troubleshooting continues.

